\section{Archived Comparative Validation on Zero-Speed Records}
\label{sec:csm-validation}
\subsection{Protocol, comparators and scoring}
This section retains the v1.2.3 stationary-vessel experiment, including its
original paired comparisons and machinery/transition traces. It is not
underway evidence. The current v1.3.0 deterministic baseline is stated
separately; its rebaselining does not transfer these confidence intervals.

The following experiments evaluate the complete shared-mode estimator,
including its proxy, magnetic acquisition, bias release and adaptation.
They use the same simulation machinery as the OU--III article: the
versioned \texttt{oceanography-waves-lib} v1.2.3 estimated 28~ft fin-keel
vessel-response records. JONSWAP and PM--Stokes each supply four stationary
incident significant heights, $H_s=0.27,1.5,4.0,8.5$~m. The response of
this one prescribed hull is not a measured sea trial or a population of
unseen vessels.
The corresponding peak periods are $T_p=3.0,5.7,8.5,11.4$~s.
Each height retains the released record's associated wave period and
direction. These are comparisons of complete sea conditions, not an
amplitude-only sweep at fixed period and direction.

Each replay lasts 1200~s at 200~Hz. The Kalman comparators use 25~Hz magnetic
input. The common corruption includes accelerometer white noise of
$1.51\times10^{-3}g$, gyro white noise of $1.57\times10^{-3}$~rad/s,
initial residual biases, and bias random walks of
$5\times10^{-4}~\mathrm{m\,s^{-2}}/\sqrt{\mathrm{s}}$ and
$10^{-5}~\mathrm{rad\,s^{-1}}/\sqrt{\mathrm{s}}$, respectively. Magnetometer noise is
0.60~$\mu$T, with residual hard iron and scale/cross-axis error. Temperature
is held at $35^\circ$C. The published simulator adapters retain their
existing sensor-covariance scales, startup and tuning coefficients; no
coefficient is retuned for the experiments reported here. Existing settings
can reflect earlier development on the same hull model.

Ten independent wave/IMU/initialization seed triplets are reused across
methods. A common random phase is applied to the world-velocity and Euler
spectra; displacement and acceleration are derived consistently from
velocity, and body specific force and gyro are rebuilt. Each method sees
the same phase-randomized motion and the same inertial-error realization
within a paired draw. The scored interval is the final 900~s, or 180000
samples. We report
\begin{equation}
 E_z=\sqrt{\langle(\hat z-z)^2\rangle},\qquad
 E_{3D}=\sqrt{\langle\|\hat{\vc p}-\vc p\|^2\rangle},
\end{equation}
and $100E/H_s$ when normalizing across heights. Attitude errors use wrapped
Euler differences; bias scores use the norm of the three-axis error.
Startup transients remain in the displayed full-run traces but are outside
the scored stationary interval.

The principal comparisons are shared CARMA, uncoupled CARMA(4,1), OU--II,
OU--III and the two-frame Lie-group filter (TFG). The uncoupled arm uses the
same six-state cascade with cross-axis excitation disabled. It retains the
27-coordinate implementation while realizing the independent CARMA law;
thus it isolates sharing without changing the frontend or spectral shape.
It is not a timing measurement of a separate 21-state implementation.

For the primary JONSWAP summary, each seed first averages the four
height-normalized errors. The ten resulting seed-level values determine
the reported mean and sample standard deviation. PM--Stokes is kept as a
separate spectral family. Paired intervals resample the ten seed-level
shared-CARMA-minus-OU--III differences 10000 times, retaining the pairing
across all four heights. These exploratory 95\% percentile intervals do
not have a multiple-endpoint correction.

Adaptive PII and the repository's time-varying-gain nonlinear observer
(TVG--NLO, based on \cite{tvg-nlo}) are additional single-draw common-output
comparisons. Their vertical displacement and tilt are scored. PII uses its
existing 20~Hz magnetic path; TVG--NLO omits compass and horizontal GNSS
aiding, so its yaw and horizontal position are unscored. These aiding
differences preclude treating all seven methods as identical sensor-fusion
architectures. In particular, a zero-filled unavailable output is never
counted as a successful estimate.

