as successful tracking; they do not demonstrate recovery of every physical
bias component. Table~\ref{tab:csm-validation-nominal} quantifies these
errors across all ten medium-sea draws.

\subsection{Comparative results}
Table~\ref{tab:csm-validation-comparison} gives the two spectral ensembles;
Fig.~\ref{fig:csm-validation-comparison} retains the individual heights.
Table~\ref{tab:csm-validation-paired} quantifies the paired contrast with
OU--III. Both heave and full three-dimensional error are reported because
an improvement in the vertical channel need not improve horizontal
reconstruction or attitude. The uncoupled arm distinguishes the contribution
of shared excitation from that of a stationary CARMA displacement prior.
The shared-CARMA-minus-OU--III heave contrast is
$\CSMJonswapHeaveDifference$ percentage points of $H_s$ for JONSWAP
(95\% interval $\CSMJonswapHeaveInterval$), and
$\CSMPmStokesHeaveDifference$ for PM--Stokes
($\CSMPmStokesHeaveInterval$). These contrasts measure the complete
estimators on the declared synthetic ensemble, not universal superiority
of a stochastic prior.
The uncoupled CARMA prior also captures much of the advantage over OU--III
on these records. The difference between the two CARMA arms isolates the
smaller additional contribution of sharing; the entire CARMA-versus-OU
difference cannot be attributed to directional coupling.
The paired shared-minus-uncoupled 3-D contrasts are
$\CSMJonswapThreeDUncoupledDifference$ percentage points for JONSWAP
($\CSMJonswapThreeDUncoupledInterval$) and
$\CSMPmStokesThreeDUncoupledDifference$ for PM--Stokes
($\CSMPmStokesThreeDUncoupledInterval$). Table~\ref{tab:csm-validation-paired}
also retains the corresponding heave contrasts.
