\section{Degradation Under Engine Vibration}
\label{sec:csm-engine}
\subsection{Sensor-path stress and controls}
The engine experiment uses the same prescribed three-cylinder four-stroke
diesel, flexible mounts, $2.6\!:\!1$ reduction gear and three-blade propeller
as the OU--III study. Crank orders, shaft/blade lines, broadband vibration,
governor variation, accelerometer rectification and gyro acceleration
sensitivity perturb the sensor path. Mount transmissibility is
\begin{equation}
 T(f)=\sqrt{\frac{1+(2\zeta r)^2}{(1-r^2)^2+(2\zeta r)^2}},\qquad r=f/f_n.
\end{equation}
The mount uses $f_n=10$~Hz and $\zeta=0.12$. The gyro terms use a 1.5~m
effective lever arm and acceleration sensitivity
$1.78\times10^{-4}$~(rad/s)/(m/s$^2$); accelerometer rectification is
1~mg per $g^2$. These are declared stress-model parameters, not fitted
measurements from the IMU installation.
The nominal condition is 2400~rpm, hull acceleration RMS 0.60~m/s$^2$, and
two-pole sensor bandwidth 80~Hz before 200~Hz sampling. The vibration model
reports recorded RMS 0.454334~m/s$^2$. Vessel rigid-body motion and magnetic
input remain unchanged; this is not a propulsion or encounter-frequency
model, and the prescribed engine installation is not a measured hull.

All eight stationary records use the first paired seed triplet. The raw
degradation arm disables both the vibration guard and its accelerometer
covariance inflation in shared CARMA, uncoupled CARMA and OU--III. The
speed sweep uses 800, 1600, 2400 and 3200~rpm. Hull levels are
0.30, 0.60 and 1.20~m/s$^2$ at cruise; sensor bandwidths are
20, 40, 80 and 160~Hz. A matched-power control rescales the hull level so
all four bandwidth cells have the nominal recorded vibration RMS. A further
cruise cell removes the modeled gyro-vibration and acceleration-sensitivity
terms while retaining the accelerometer perturbation.

Every error is scored at full rate over the final 900~s. An engine-table
RMS pools records as $\sqrt{\frac18\sum_{j=1}^8 E_j^2}$; it is not the
arithmetic mean of the record RMS values. The displacement offset pools
the norm of each record's mean error. This separates persistent error
from fluctuating error without implying that either has a unique physical
cause. The single engine draw per record supplies a controlled stress
comparison, not an ensemble confidence interval.

