\subsection{Fixed mitigation settings}
The mitigation arms use the existing two-pole 14~Hz guard, followed either
by the ordinary accelerometer covariance or by the existing vibration-aware
gain 0.75. Engagement uses the common two-pole 25~Hz high-pass detector,
0.03--0.08~m/s$^2$ rails and 5~s engagement scale. Thus the three arms are
raw input, guard only, and guard plus covariance; coefficients and quality
thresholds are not retuned for this experiment.
Table~\ref{tab:csm-validation-engine} includes the nominal condition and
engine-off baseline. Figure~\ref{fig:csm-validation-engine-speed} shows
whether the mitigation holds across speed. An improvement in displacement
must be considered together with pitch and yaw: rejecting high-frequency
input can change phase delay and the distribution of the accelerometer
residual among tilt, bias and inferred translation.
At nominal cruise shared-CARMA 3-D RMS changes from the engine-off
$\CSMEngineBaselineThreeD$~m to $\CSMEngineRawThreeD$~m on raw vibration
(a factor $\CSMEngineRawRatio$). The fixed guard-plus-covariance arm gives
$\CSMEngineDeployedThreeD$~m, or $\CSMEngineDeployedRatio$ times the
engine-off value. The speed and bandwidth controls show why this one
condition is insufficient to establish robustness over an installation.
Guard alone gives $\CSMEngineGuardOnlyThreeD$~m at nominal cruise. Thus,
in this condition, the fixed covariance-inflation gain further reduces
shared-CARMA 3-D error. The benefit is not uniform across outputs:
at 800~rpm it increases pitch RMS from $\CSMEngineIdleGuardPitch$ to
$\CSMEngineIdleDeployedPitch$ degrees. The common gain is retained rather
than optimized on these evaluation records.
Removing the modeled gyro perturbation changes nominal shared-CARMA 3-D
RMS by $\CSMEngineGyroPathChange\%$ while the accelerometer perturbation is
retained. This attributes error within the declared machinery model; it
does not assert insensitivity to arbitrary gyro errors.
\CSMValidationEngineTable

\section{Low--High--Low Sea-State Transition}
\label{sec:csm-roundtrip}
The bidirectional test exactly reuses the OU--III transition construction.
The low sea has $(H_s,T_p)=(1.5~\mathrm{m},5.7~\mathrm{s})$. A quintic
$C^2$ crossfade begins at 400~s and reaches the independent high-sea
realization $(4.0~\mathrm{m},11.4~\mathrm{s})$ at 520~s. The high sea is
held to 800~s; an identical 120~s crossfade returns to the \emph{same}
phase-randomized low-sea realization at 920~s.

For a blending weight $w$, consistent kinematics require
\begin{align}
 \vc p&=(1-w)\vc p_L+w\vc p_H,\nonumber\\
 \vc v&=(1-w)\vc v_L+w\vc v_H+\dot w(\vc p_H-\vc p_L),\nonumber\\
 \vc a&=(1-w)\vc a_L+w\vc a_H
       +2\dot w(\vc v_H-\vc v_L)\nonumber\\
      &\quad+\ddot w(\vc p_H-\vc p_L).
\end{align}
Attitude is blended and body IMU channels are rebuilt. No state,
covariance, bias, magnetic reference, adaptation memory or coupling monitor
is reset at either transition. Shared CARMA, uncoupled CARMA and OU--III
each run one continuous replay for each of the ten common seed triplets.

The scored window is divided into low-start (300--400~s), rise
(400--520~s), high-recovery (520--640~s), high-settled (640--800~s), fall
(800--920~s), low-recovery (920--1040~s), and low-return (1040--1200~s).
Table~\ref{tab:csm-validation-roundtrip} reports heave and three-dimensional
RMS in every interval. Separate rise and fall scores prevent opposite
adaptation lags from being hidden in a pooled transition average. Unequal
window lengths, phase and evolving sensor bias mean that low-start and
low-return errors need not coincide even though the low-sea realization
is the same.
For shared CARMA, the ten-seed low-start, high-settled and low-return heave
RMS means are $\CSMTransitionLowStartHeave$, $\CSMTransitionHighHeave$ and
$\CSMTransitionLowReturnHeave$~m. The rise, fall and recovery scores remain
visible in the table rather than being replaced by a single aggregate.
\CSMValidationRoundtripTable

